  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAn Omega Block will continue across the Lower 48 through the\nrest of the holiday weekend, characterized by mid/upper troughs\nover the Pacific Northwest and eastern Great Lakes/New England\nand a broad mid/upper ridge in between over the Plains. This\nwill keep the southern Great Lakes and Ohio Valley under\nfavorable upper level confluence to maintain a large surface\nhigh of Canadian origin overhead. As a result, the very pleasant\ntemperatures and low humidity found across the area this \nafternoon will continue through Labor Day Monday. Expect another\ncool night tonight, with lows in the low/mid 50s inland from the\nlake and upper 50s to near 60 along the lakeshore. A slightly\nwarmer day is expected Monday, but still comfortable with highs\nin the mid 70s to around 80.\n\nThe large Canadian high will drift east into the Mid Atlantic\nregion Monday night and Tuesday as the central CONUS mid/upper\nridge expands east into the Ohio and Tennessee Valleys in\nresponse to shortwave energy ejecting into the northern Plains.\nThis will set up return southerly flow and resultant warm air\nadvection, so temperatures and dew points will be noticeably\nhigher Tuesday. Look for highs to get back into the low/mid 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe mid/upper ridge will become firmly established across the\nOhio and Tennessee Valleys (briefly) Tuesday night and Wednesday\nas the aforementioned shortwave progresses into the Great Lakes.\nStrong warm air advection ahead of the accompanying cold front\nand 850 mb temps warming to 17-18 C beneath the thermal ridge\nwill support highs in the upper 80s to around 90 Wednesday, and\ndew points in the low 70s will yield heat indices in the \nlow/mid 90s. The ridge will already be flattening Wednesday \nevening as the shortwave digs into the central Great Lakes. This\nwill push the cold front through the region late Wednesday \nevening or Wednesday night. There will likely be a narrow ribbon\nof moderate to strong instability given the heat and humidity, \nand this coupled with the right entrance region of a 120-130 \nknot upper jet streak crossing Lower Michigan should support \norganized convection. Will need to monitor for strong to severe \nstorms Wednesday evening into Wednesday night, but the main \nuncertainty continues to be convective coverage. A second \nmid/upper shortwave crossing the Great Lakes Thursday will \nlikely stall the cold frontal boundary over northern or central \nOhio in response to a surface wave tracking along it, and this \nwill keep decent coverage of showers and thunderstorms over the \narea Thursday before drying out Thursday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nCanadian high pressure will finally build into the southern\nGreat Lakes and Ohio Valley Thursday night and Friday and push \nthe boundary south of the area, supporting cooler and less humid\nconditions. However, renewed mid/upper troughing pushing into \nthe western CONUS next weekend will build and expand the\nstubborn central CONUS mid/upper ridge back into the Great Lakes\nand Ohio Valley. This will cause heat and humidity to build back\ninto the area next weekend, with highs in the 70s Friday warming\ninto at least the mid 80s for the weekend.",
